 It is certainly worth mentioning that although alanine appears to be fraught with poorly predicted interactions, \(91 \%\) of all interactions are predicted with below a \(1 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\) error and \(99.3 \%\) below 1 kcal \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) error. In fact, the mean electrostatic energy prediction error for individual interatomic interactions across the entire set of alanine interactions is only \(0.006 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\).

It would appear that even in the worst-predicted amino acids, the vast majority of predictions deliver highly accurate electrostatic energies. However, so far we have only modeled neutral amino acids and perhaps modeling charged side chains is equally as important, given their prevalence in biochemical reactions. We have, in the past, shown kriging's ability to model
aromatic side chains, \({ }^{99}\) and it has been shown here that amino acids with aromatic side chains do not stand out in their prediction errors when compared to other amino acids. In addition to this observation, we present a set of five charged amino acids as indicated by a green arrow in Figure 1. Each charged amino acid is created by adding or removing a hydrogen atom in the distorted geometries; these hydrogen positions are indicated by the green arrows in Figure 1. Each set of charged amino acids has a geometrically identical set of neutral amino acids with exception to that single mentioned hydrogen atom. Thus, is it fair to compare these two sets of geometries and their predictions in order to find any additional error introduced by the amino acids being non-neutral. Table 3

